{Commentary of al-Baydawi (Anwar al-Tanzil) on surah 11, verse 108:}

﴿And as for those who are made happy, they will be in the Garden, abiding therein for as long as the heavens and the earth endure, except what your Lord wills — a gift never cut off.﴾ (p-150) "Never cut off" means not severed. This is an explicit statement that the reward does not come to an end, and an indication that the exception mentioned concerning the reward does not mean cessation. For this reason he distinguished between reward and punishment by making the former everlasting (ta'bid).

Hamzah, al-Kisa'i and Hafs read ﴿suʿidū﴾ in the passive voice, from saʿadahu Allah, meaning asʿadahu (made him happy). The word ﴿ʿaṭāʾan﴾ is in the accusative as an emphatic infinitive (maṣdar mu'akkid), meaning "they were given a gift," or it is a circumstantial accusative (ḥāl) referring to "the Garden."
